the timeout issue with safe=T has been fixed, and if its goes out on
timeout then it will now and not try to 'fix it'.  (version 22.04.06)

-----Original Message-----
From: ecogr...@gmail.com <ecogr...@gmail.com>

To: R-inla discussion group <r-inla-disc...@googlegroups.com>
Subject: Re: [r-inla] inla seems to restart when killed

Date: Tue, 5 Apr 2022 11:30:03 -0700 (PDT)

Does the switch to safe=F have implications for memory?  I encountered the inla.core.safe/restart issue yesterday, likely due to a time out, but after switching to safe=F today I'm now exceeding memory on models previously run with the default safe=T.  Previously run models and those now exceeding memory used INLA_22.03.28, pardiso,  inla.mode="experimental", strategy="adaptive" , and the same initial values.
Thanks.

On Tuesday, April 5, 2022 at 8:45:26 AM UTC-6 he...@r-inla.org wrote:
>

> ``safe=F'' was the default behaviour before it was added in
> December'21.
> with safe=T, then if the model fails to fit, f.ex, it will try various
> tricks to improved the initial values and make it work. previously,
> this
> had to be done manually.
>
> I see the issue with timeout, which should be fixed.
>
